 * Portal: `/api/ui` answers at most 8 requests at once; more get 503 `busy`. The count "refused at the door" on the Overview page (requests the Host and Origin check turned away; not the same as the `door` of a lead, `form` or `webhook`) is kept in memory and starts again at 0 when the desk restarts: read it as "since desk start (HH:MM)".
 * Threads: the desk serves each connection on its own thread. Writing a reply has a total limit of `REPLY_WRITE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (5 s): a client that does not read its reply loses the thread after that.
 * Open files: at start-up the desk checks that the open-file limit of this computer covers `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (4 files per thread plus 64 for everything else; contract.md lists what the 4 and the 64 are made of). With the default 1024 threads that is 4160 open files. A computer whose hard limit is lower than that now makes the desk exit with code 3; 4096 is a common default inside containers, so there raise the limit (for example `ulimit -n 8192`, or the container's own open-file setting) or lower `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS`. The count is arithmetic, checked by measuring a database connection and the portal's connections; DNS lookups and the temporary files SQLite makes are covered by the 64 only roughly (not measured, not itemised). It raises the soft limit to exactly what is needed when the hard limit is unlimited or at least that, and otherwise refuses to start (exit code 3). The message tells you either to lower `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` to a number it names (only when a setting of 8 or more fits the limit in force) or, when no allowed setting fits, to raise the open-file limit (`ulimit -n`).
-* Exit codes: 1 the sender cannot be reached; 2 another desk holds the lock file or the port cannot be bound; 3 the open-file limit is too low; 4 the desk refuses a setting when it is built (an AI adapter whose `timeout_seconds` is not below the stuck grace minus its margin; the message says so; no other error at that step gets code 4, and a constant edited to a value that does not fit is a Python traceback, not an exit code).
+* Exit codes: 1 the sender cannot be reached, or a Python traceback (a constant edited to a value that does not fit); 2 another desk holds the lock file or the port cannot be bound; 3 the open-file limit is too low; 4 the desk refuses a setting when it is built (an AI adapter whose `timeout_seconds` is not a number above 0, or is not below the stuck grace minus its margin; the message says so; no other error at that step gets code 4).
 * Stopping: Ctrl+C or SIGTERM stop the background job first and then give the lock file back. A second signal while that runs is ignored on purpose, so it cannot cut a step short. If the desk is still there after about 10 seconds, end it with SIGKILL (on Windows, end the process); the lock is dropped and the next start puts every unfinished follow-up back.
 * Two limits apply. (1) At most `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (256) connections can be in their reading phase (request line, headers, body) at one time; a connection stops counting when the desk starts writing its reply, so a thread that is still finishing a follow-up does not count here. (2) At most `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (1024) handler threads exist in total: a thread counts from the moment it gets a connection until it ends, so the reply write, the draining and the follow-up count too. When either limit is reached and nothing can be closed to make room, the new connection gets 503 `busy` and nothing is stored.
 * Deadlines: request line plus headers must arrive within `HEADER_READ_DEADLINE_SECONDS` and the body within `BODY_READ_DEADLINE_SECONDS` (10 s each, measured in real time from the start of that phase, however slowly the bytes trickle in); a stalled sender gets 408 `{"error":"request_timeout","receipt_id":null}`, and nothing was stored, so the same request id can be sent again.
